WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:01.440
SAAD ALI: My name is Saad Ali.

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:02.920
I'm a software
engineer at Google.

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:04.740
And today, I have
here with me Janet.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.550
Janet is the co-chair of
the Kubernetes KubeCon

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:11.850
for Seattle this year
as well as for China.

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:13.240
JANET KUO: Yeah, that's right.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:15.360
SAAD ALI: So Janet, how
did you get involved with,

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.070
let's start with Kubernetes.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.236
How did you get involved
with the Kubernetes project

00:00:19.236 --> 00:00:20.320
to begin with?

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.570
JANET KUO: So I
started with Kubernetes

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:25.210
as my first project at Google.

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:27.330
So in the beginning,
I was interviewing

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:28.800
at a bunch of places.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.890
And then I got a phone call from
Google, and it's Tim Hockin.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:35.850
He's a principal software
engineer at Google,

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:40.230
and I had a brief
conversation with him.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:43.950
And he just told me how
great Kubernetes is,

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.330
and it's going to
change the world,

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:49.020
and it's still very,
very new at that time.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:55.020
So I just say, I just think,
I decided to jump in the ship

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:58.089
and go with the Kubernetes team.

00:00:58.089 --> 00:00:59.130
SAAD ALI: That's awesome.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.410
And how long ago was that?

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:03.060
JANET KUO: It's three years ago.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:03.540
SAAD ALI: Three years.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:04.110
Congrats.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:07.230
So since then, you've
been an avid contributor

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:08.825
in the Kubernetes community.

00:01:08.825 --> 00:01:10.950
What are some of the things
that you've contributed

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:13.730
to over the last three years?

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:16.290
JANET KUO: So I'm in the
applications special interest

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:17.070
group.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:21.600
So we mainly focusing on
Kubernetes applications

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.700
and your experience running
applications in Kubernetes.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.960
So the workload's API,
and like deployment

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:36.210
API that most people are using,
and the StatefulSet, DaemonSet,

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:38.370
and jobs, current jobs.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.140
Those kind of stuffs.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:43.710
And recently, I was working
on improving the garbage

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:45.870
collection of resources.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:50.520
Because I observed that a
lot of users have resources

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:54.240
in their clusters, but they
cannot clean them up very

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:54.820
easily.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.220
So I want to improve
that part as well.

00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:58.470
SAAD ALI: That sounds awesome.

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:01.590
So it sounds like recently you
switched gears a little bit

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:04.140
and are taking on
the task of leading,

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:09.360
putting together KubeCon, which
this one is 8,000 attendees.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.259
What is that like?

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:11.970
How did you get involved?

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.260
And what can you
tell us about that?

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:16.800
JANET KUO: So a lot of people
ask me the same question

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.270
about how I get involved.

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:21.090
And how can they sign up.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:23.790
But actually, I didn't
sign up, and I just

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.370
got an email from Dan Kohn.

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:29.100
He's the executive of CNCF.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:32.670
He just asked me,
hey, Janet, would you

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:35.770
like to be the
co-chair of KubeCon?

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:38.400
I was like, oh,
my god, of course.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:38.940
Yes!

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:39.690
SAAD ALI: That is awesome.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:40.200
JANET KUO: Yeah.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.658
SAAD ALI: Were you
hesitant at all?

00:02:41.658 --> 00:02:43.770
Or did you have any
concerns going in?

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:45.660
Or were you just excited?

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:47.040
JANET KUO: I'm just too excited.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.920
And then later, I
figured out, oh, there's

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:53.250
a lot of things going on there.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:57.240
A lot of those
things that I need

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:00.840
to do that normal attendees
wouldn't know there's

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:01.889
a lot of work there.

00:03:01.889 --> 00:03:03.930
SAAD ALI: And so can you
talk about some of that?

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:06.221
What are some of the things
that our audience might not

00:03:06.221 --> 00:03:08.730
know about that goes
into putting together

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.330
an event like this?

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:13.510
JANET KUO: So first, we
need to review all the CFPs.

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:17.040
So CFP is like a
proposal for your talk.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:19.980
So we need to review
all of them and pick

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:23.880
the ones that we think
that the community can most

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.410
benefit from.

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:32.370
And also, we'll pick some of
the talks as a keynote speeches.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:35.760
And also, we'll invite
some industry leaders

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.950
to give keynotes.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:42.000
We think they'll bring
some new ideas to us.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.220
And then co-chairs
need to do also do MC.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:50.810
And we also have a chance
to do our own keynotes.

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:51.870
And that's--

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:54.966
SAAD ALI: You gave your first
keynote this morning, right?

00:03:54.966 --> 00:03:56.340
JANET KUO: Oh, I
haven't give it.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:56.760
SAAD ALI: Oh, I'm sorry.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:57.500
OK.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:58.440
That will be tomorrow.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:03:58.710
JANET KUO: Yeah.

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:00.597
That will be tomorrow
and on Thursday.

00:04:00.597 --> 00:04:01.930
SAAD ALI: That's super exciting.

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:05.070
Are you ready to be in front of
the audience of 8,000 people?

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:05.974
JANET KUO: Yeah.

00:04:05.974 --> 00:04:07.140
SAAD ALI: That's super cool.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:10.500
So what are some of the
things that didn't quite

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:15.390
go as expected that maybe you
would suggest improvements

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:16.952
on in the future?

00:04:16.952 --> 00:04:17.910
JANET KUO: For KubeCon?

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:20.910
SAAD ALI: For KubeCon,
event planning, any of that.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:23.580
JANET KUO: So for example,
I did the co-chair

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:26.430
for both Shanghai and Seattle.

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:28.950
And at that time,
because Shanghai

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:33.150
is the first one in Asia, so
we did a bit of experiment.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:36.090
For example, we tried
to have people submit

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:39.480
to both conferences at once.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:43.500
But then later we figured
that caused some confusion

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:44.260
to people.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:46.330
So we are not going
to do that next year.

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:51.330
So if you want to submit CFP,
you submit to each conference

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:53.160
individually.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:53.910
SAAD ALI: Got it.

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:55.110
That's super cool.

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:58.470
And so outside of KubeCon,
what are you looking forward

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:00.470
to in the next year in
the Kubernetes community

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:02.460
or anything at large?

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:08.000
JANET KUO: So I think serverless
and Service Mesh is taking off,

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.250
and a lot of people are building
applications of Kubernetes.

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:14.840
They are writing
their own operators

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:17.570
to operate their
own applications.

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:21.650
And also, people are building
developer tools for you

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:27.690
to easily create your
operators and things like that.

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:30.650
So I'm very excited
about how people

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:32.504
build on top of Kubernetes.

00:05:32.504 --> 00:05:34.670
SAAD ALI: And make it easier
to deploy applications.

00:05:34.670 --> 00:05:35.750
JANET KUO: Yeah, exactly.

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:36.800
SAAD ALI: Well,
that sounds awesome.

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:38.750
Thank you very much
for your time, Janet.

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:42.604
And have a great rest
of your KubeCon here.

00:05:42.604 --> 00:05:43.520
This is a great event.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:44.065
Thank you.

00:05:44.065 --> 00:05:44.940
JANET KUO: Thank you.

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:45.140
SAAD ALI: All right.

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:45.920
Take care.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:48.727
[MUSIC PLAYING]

